AUREUS-000 — DOCUMENT 36

CONSTITUTIONAL INTERPRETATION AND AMENDMENT

Status: Constitutional Document

---

Purpose

This document establishes how the Constitution of Aureus shall be interpreted, amended, and preserved.

The Constitution must be stable enough to protect the institution while remaining capable of responsible improvement.

Amendment exists to strengthen the Constitution—not to weaken its identity.

---

Section 1 — Constitutional Supremacy

This Constitution is the highest governing authority of Aureus.

Every policy, standard, procedure, technology, agreement, and institutional action shall conform to it.

If any lower governing document conflicts with this Constitution, the Constitution prevails.

---

Section 2 — Principles of Interpretation

Every constitutional interpretation shall seek to preserve the Constitution as a unified whole.

Interpretations shall:

- uphold the Mission of Aureus;
- preserve the Eternal Principles;
- protect the Rights of Every Member;
- favor stewardship over institutional convenience;
- avoid interpretations that create internal contradiction.

No provision shall be interpreted in isolation when doing so would undermine the Constitution as a whole.

---

Section 3 — Constitutional Interpretations

Questions concerning the meaning or application of this Constitution shall be resolved through written constitutional interpretations.

Each interpretation shall identify:

- the constitutional provisions considered;
- the question presented;
- the reasoning supporting the interpretation;
- the resulting guidance.

Interpretations clarify the Constitution.

They do not amend it.

All constitutional interpretations shall become part of the permanent constitutional record.

---

Section 4 — Constitutional Review

The Constitution shall be reviewed periodically to identify:

- ambiguities;
- inconsistencies;
- operational lessons;
- emerging risks;
- opportunities for responsible improvement.

A constitutional review does not modify the Constitution unless an amendment is lawfully adopted.

---

Section 5 — Amendment Principles

Amendments shall:

- strengthen constitutional clarity;
- improve institutional stewardship;
- preserve member trust;
- maintain internal consistency;
- respect the enduring identity of Aureus.

No amendment shall be adopted merely for convenience or short-term advantage.

---

Section 6 — The Unamendable Core

The following constitutional commitments are permanent and shall not be repealed or substantially diminished:

- the Mission of Aureus;
- the inherent dignity of every person;
- stewardship as the exercise of authority;
- the Rights of Every Member;
- truthfulness;
- respect for human agency;
- the principle that people are never products;
- the commitment to help people flourish.

Future amendments may clarify these principles but shall not abolish or contradict them.

---

Section 7 — Amendment Process

Every proposed constitutional amendment shall:

1. clearly identify the provisions affected;
2. explain the purpose of the amendment;
3. describe its expected institutional impact;
4. be publicly recorded within the constitutional record before adoption.

Approved amendments shall receive a version number, adoption date, and permanent archival record.

---

Section 8 — Constitutional Record

Aureus shall preserve a permanent constitutional record containing:

- every ratified constitutional document;
- every amendment;
- constitutional interpretations;
- historical versions;
- ratification records;
- constitutional stewardship reports.

No adopted constitutional history shall be erased.

Superseded provisions shall remain archived for historical transparency.

---

Section 9 — Constitutional Continuity

Each generation of Aureus inherits this Constitution as a steward rather than an owner.

Future leaders possess the responsibility to preserve its identity while responsibly strengthening its application.

The Constitution belongs to the institution, not to any individual generation.

---

Section 10 — Final Standard

Every constitutional interpretation and every proposed amendment shall ultimately answer one question:

"Does this strengthen Aureus' ability to faithfully help people flourish—forever—while preserving the dignity, rights, and trust of every person we serve?"